2025-05-01 02:42:27 - Thread-947 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and how they would be used  

• "_1000_GENOMES_PHASE_3_VARIANTS_20150220"."alternate_bases": to check how many alternate alleles exist at every position (we want SIZE = 1).  
• "_1000_GENOMES_PHASE_3_VARIANTS_20150220"."call": JSON array that holds one element per sample; from each element we will extract  
  – "name"  →  sample ID to be counted  
  – "genotype"  →  array of the two alleles; we keep rows where genotype[0]=0 and genotype[1]=0 (homozygous reference).  
• "_1000_GENOMES_PHASE_3_VARIANTS_20150220"."reference_name" / "start_position": site identifiers (only for inspection).  
• "_1000_GENOMES_SAMPLE_INFO"."Sample", "Population": optional enrichment of the TOP‑10 list with population information.  

Typical conditions to apply  

1. ARRAY_SIZE("alternate_bases") = 1    → exactly one ALT allele.  
2. ("genotype"[0]::INT = 0 AND "genotype"[1]::INT = 0)    → homozygous reference.  

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Example Snowflake SQLs (each limited to 20 rows for safety)

```sql
-- 1) Quick look at how many ALT alleles each record carries
SELECT  v."reference_name",
        v."start_position",
        ARRAY_SIZE(v."alternate_bases") AS "alt_count"
FROM    HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS.HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS."_1000_GENOMES_PHASE_3_VARIANTS_20150220" v
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Show the actual ALT allele strings after flattening the array
SELECT  v."reference_name",
        v."start_position",
        a.value:"alt"::STRING AS "alt"
FROM    HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS.HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS."_1000_GENOMES_PHASE_3_VARIANTS_20150220" v,
        LATERAL FLATTEN(input => v."alternate_bases") a
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Explore the FORMAT of the per‑sample JSON “call” field
SELECT  c.value:"name"::STRING      AS "sample",
        c.value:"genotype"          AS "raw_genotype",
        c.value:"genotype"[0]::INT  AS "allele_1",
        c.value:"genotype"[1]::INT  AS "allele_2"
FROM    HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS.HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS."_1000_GENOMES_PHASE_3_VARIANTS_20150220" v,
        LATERAL FLATTEN(input => v."call") c
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Count how often each genotype combination occurs
SELECT  c.value:"genotype"[0]::INT  AS "allele_1",
        c.value:"genotype"[1]::INT  AS "allele_2",
        COUNT(*)                    AS "num_calls"
FROM    HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS.HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS."_1000_GENOMES_PHASE_3_VARIANTS_20150220" v,
        LATERAL FLATTEN(input => v."call") c
GROUP BY 1,2
ORDER BY "num_calls" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Pull a sample of sites where there is only ONE ALT allele AND the sample is hom‑ref (0/0)
SELECT  v."reference_name",
        v."start_position",
        c.value:"name"::STRING AS "sample"
FROM    HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS.HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS."_1000_GENOMES_PHASE_3_VARIANTS_20150220" v,
        LATERAL FLATTEN(input => v."call") c
WHERE   ARRAY_SIZE(v."alternate_bases") = 1
  AND   c.value:"genotype"[0]::INT = 0
  AND   c.value:"genotype"[1]::INT = 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Count (UNORDERED) how many hom‑ref‑single‑alt positions each sample owns
SELECT  c.value:"name"::STRING AS "sample",
        COUNT(*)               AS "hom_ref_single_alt_sites"
FROM    HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS.HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS."_1000_GENOMES_PHASE_3_VARIANTS_20150220" v,
        LATERAL FLATTEN(input => v."call") c
WHERE   ARRAY_SIZE(v."alternate_bases") = 1
  AND   c.value:"genotype"[0]::INT = 0
  AND   c.value:"genotype"[1]::INT = 0
GROUP BY 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Same as #6 but ordered so that the biggest counts show up first
SELECT  c.value:"name"::STRING AS "sample",
        COUNT(*)               AS "hom_ref_single_alt_sites"
FROM    HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS.HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS."_1000_GENOMES_PHASE_3_VARIANTS_20150220" v,
        LATERAL FLATTEN(input => v."call") c
WHERE   ARRAY_SIZE(v."alternate_bases") = 1
  AND   c.value:"genotype"[0]::INT = 0
  AND   c.value:"genotype"[1]::INT = 0
GROUP BY 1
ORDER BY "hom_ref_single_alt_sites" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Narrow to the true TOP‑10 by count
SELECT  c.value:"name"::STRING AS "sample",
        COUNT(*)               AS "hom_ref_single_alt_sites"
FROM    HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS.HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS."_1000_GENOMES_PHASE_3_VARIANTS_20150220" v,
        LATERAL FLATTEN(input => v."call") c
WHERE   ARRAY_SIZE(v."alternate_bases") = 1
  AND   c.value:"genotype"[0]::INT = 0
  AND   c.value:"genotype"[1]::INT = 0
GROUP BY 1
ORDER BY "hom_ref_single_alt_sites" DESC
LIMIT 10;
```

```sql
-- 9) Join the TOP‑10 list to sample‑level meta‑data to see population information
WITH top10 AS (
  SELECT  c.value:"name"::STRING AS "sample",
          COUNT(*)               AS "hom_ref_single_alt_sites"
  FROM    HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS.HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS."_1000_GENOMES_PHASE_3_VARIANTS_20150220" v,
          LATERAL FLATTEN(input => v."call") c
  WHERE   ARRAY_SIZE(v."alternate_bases") = 1
    AND   c.value:"genotype"[0]::INT = 0
    AND   c.value:"genotype"[1]::INT = 0
  GROUP BY 1
  ORDER BY "hom_ref_single_alt_sites" DESC
  LIMIT 10
)
SELECT  t."sample",
        t."hom_ref_single_alt_sites",
        si."Population"
FROM    top10 t
LEFT JOIN HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS.HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS."_1000_GENOMES_SAMPLE_INFO" si
  ON t."sample" = si."Sample"
ORDER BY t."hom_ref_single_alt_sites" DESC;
```

```sql
--10) Same core logic as #9, also showing the super‑population if desired
WITH top10 AS (
  SELECT  c.value:"name"::STRING AS "sample",
          COUNT(*)               AS "hom_ref_single_alt_sites"
  FROM    HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS.HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS."_1000_GENOMES_PHASE_3_VARIANTS_20150220" v,
          LATERAL FLATTEN(input => v."call") c
  WHERE   ARRAY_SIZE(v."alternate_bases") = 1
    AND   c.value:"genotype"[0]::INT = 0
    AND   c.value:"genotype"[1]::INT = 0
  GROUP BY 1
  ORDER BY "hom_ref_single_alt_sites" DESC
  LIMIT 10
)
SELECT  t."sample",
        t."hom_ref_single_alt_sites",
        si."Population",
        si."Super_Population"
FROM    top10 t
LEFT JOIN HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS.HUMAN_GENOME_VARIANTS."_1000_GENOMES_SAMPLE_INFO" si
  ON t."sample" = si."Sample"
ORDER BY t."hom_ref_single_alt_sites" DESC;
```

Each query is enclosed in ```sql``` and every identifier is wrapped in double‑quotes to spare case‑sensitivity issues. These progressively build from exploring the raw structure to delivering the requested “TOP‑10 samples by homozygous‑reference‑single‑ALT positions” result.
